[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is NAT on the border router r1. End-user subnets (10.x) reach the core (csw1/csw2) and default to r1 via OSPF, but r1 is not translating private source addresses to a public IP before sending traffic to the ISP. Evidence: r1 has IP forwarding enabled and accepts forwarding (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1; iptables FORWARD policy ACCEPT), and it has working upstream connectivity (r1 successfully pings ISPA 203.0.113.1 and public IPs 216.239.35.4, 66.235.200.145). However, end hosts (pc1/pc2/pc3) cannot reach those same public IPs (pings/curl time out). Critically, r1’s NAT table shows no outbound SNAT/MASQUERADE rules (iptables -t nat -vnL shows only an unrelated DNAT and no POSTROUTING SNAT), so packets sourced from 10.x are forwarded out eth2/eth3 with private source IPs and are dropped on return by the Internet—manifesting as TCP connection timeouts while DNS (to internal 10.5.0.4) still works.

Localization: r1, iptables NAT (POSTROUTING), interfaces eth2/eth3 toward ISPs. Both ISP links are up and routable, so this is not an ISP-side outage.

Root cause: The outbound NAT (SNAT/MASQUERADE) configuration on r1 is missing/was removed.

Corrective fix: Reinstall NAT on r1, for example:
- iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.0.0.0/8 -o eth2 -j MASQUERADE
- iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.0.0.0/8 -o eth3 -j MASQUERADE
Persist via netfilter-persistent or equivalent. After this, internal clients’ TCP sessions to the Internet should complete normally. === END ===